                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\begin{array}{l}
                      G_4^s
                      \\;=\;
                      \\tfrac{1}{4!}
                      (G_4)_{a_1 \cdots a_4}
                      e^{a_1} \cdots e^{a_4}
                      \\,-\,
                      \\tfrac{1}{2}
                      \\big(\overline{\psi}\Gamma_{a_1 a_2} \psi\big)
                      e^{a_1} \, e^{a_2}
                      \\\
                      G_7^s
                      \\;=\;
                      \\tfrac{1}{7!}
                      (G_7)_{a_1 \cdots a_7}
                      e^{a_1} \cdots e^{a_7}
                      \\,-\,
                      \\tfrac{1}{5!}
                      \\big(\overline{\psi}\Gamma_{a_1 \cdots a_5} \psi\big)
                      e^{a_1} \cdots e^{a_5}
                      \\mathrlap{\,.}
                      \\end{array}
          %p
            Up to some mild (but suggestive, see below) re-arrangement, the computation is essentially that indicated in
            %a{:href => "D'Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#CastellaniDAuriaFre"} CDF91, §III.8.5
            (where some of the easy checks are indicated) which in turn is a mild reformulation of the original claim in
            %a{:href => "#D=11+supergravity#CremmerFerrara80"} Cremmer &amp; Ferrara 1980
            and
            %a{:href => "D=11+supergravity#BrinkHowe80"} Brink &amp; Howe 1980
            (where less details were given). A full proof is laid out in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "D=11+supergravity#GiotopoulosSatiSchreiber24"} GSS24, §3
          %p
            The following may be understood as an exposition of this result, which seems to stand out as the only account that is (i) fully
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+formulation+of+gravity"} first-order
            and (ii)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality-symmetric+higher+gauge+theory"} duality-symmetric
            (in that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 7
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_7
            enters the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} EoMs
            as an independent field, whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+duality"} Hodge duality
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_4
            is imposed
            %em
              by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identity"} Bianchi identity
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi G
                      %mn 7
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_7^s
          %p
            Notice that the discussion in
            %a{:href => "D'Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#CastellaniDAuriaFre"} CDF91, §III.8
            amplifies the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superspace"} superspace
            %a{:href => "D'Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#Rheonomy"} rheonomy principle
            as a constraint that makes the Bianchi identities on (in our paraphrase) a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+6-algebra"} supergravity Lie 6-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra+valued+differential+form"} valued
            higher vielbein be equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+SuGra"} D=11 SuGra
            %em constraint
            necessary is that
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:LocalFormOfSuperFluxDensities"} (2)
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} C-field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+density"} flux density
            — and this is the one not strictly given by rules in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "D'Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#CastellaniDAuriaFre"} CDF91, p. 874
            %a{:href => "D'Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#CastellaniDAuriaFre"} CDF91, (III.8.41)
            —; while the remaining rheonomy condition on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitino"} gravitino
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            is
            %em implied
            (Lem.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SuperBianchiIdentityOnG4InComponents"}
            below), and the
            %a{:href => "torsion+constraints+in+supergravity#Examples11dSuGra"} all-important
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+constraints+in+supergravity"} torsion constraint
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TorsionConstraint"} (3)
            (which is also outside the rules of rheonomy constraints, cf.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "D'Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#CastellaniDAuriaFre"} CDF91, (III.8.33)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-spacetime"} super-spacetime
            in the first place (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SuperSpacetime"}
            below).
          %p In thus recasting the formulation of the theorem somewhat, we also:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                re-define the super-flux densities as above
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:LocalFormOfSuperFluxDensities"} (2)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identity"} Bianchi identity
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:SuperFluxBianchiIdentity"} (1)
                extends to
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superspace"} superspace
            %li
              %p
                disregard the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potentials"} gauge potentials
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_3
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 6
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_6
                %a{:href => "D'Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#CastellaniDAuriaFre"} CDF91, §III.8.2-4
                is really just to motivate the form of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identities"} Bianchi identities
                equivalent to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:SuperFluxBianchiIdentity"} (1)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
          %p
            Indeed, the point is that, in consequence of our second item above, the following formulation shows that one may apply
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} flux quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superspace"} superspace
            in formally the same way as bosonically (for instance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
            as per
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Hypothesis+H"} Hypothesis H
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology+theory"} nonabelian cohomology theory
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            has the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+L-infinity+algebra"}
              Whitehead
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+n-sphere"} of the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4-sphere"} 4-sphere
            %em implies
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} EoMs
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"} 11d SuGra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} flux quantization
            is then what defines, conversely, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potentials"} gauge potentials
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+spacetime"} super spacetime
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            For the present formulation, we find it suggestive to regard the
            %a{:href => "torsion+constraints+in+supergravity#Examples11dSuGra"} all-important
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+constraints+in+supergravity"} torsion constraint
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TorsionConstraint"} (3)
            as part of the definition of the super-gravity field itself (since it ties the auxiliary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-connection"} spin-connection
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-vielbein"} super-vielbein
            field which embodies the actual super-metric structure):
